diagnosis_text:
The DC CLOS BGP fabric is fully operational at time of investigation. All five FRR routers (two leaves, two spines, one super-spine) have established BGP sessions, and routing tables on every device are correct. Both leaf subnets (10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24) are reachable end-to-end: bidirectional ping between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) completes with 0% packet loss, and traceroute confirms the correct 4-hop forwarding path through spine_router_0_0. No firewall rules exist on any device, all physical links are UP, and BGP loop-prevention DENIED entries in the logs are expected CLOS behaviour (own-AS in AS-PATH) rather than errors.

The only notable event is that all FRR daemons were restarted simultaneously at 14:25:53 (hosts had been up ~5.5 h, FRR only ~6 min at investigation time). BGP reconverged within approximately 2 seconds of the restart, meaning there was a short window — roughly coinciding with "the night" the user reported — during which all inter-rack routes were temporarily withdrawn and traffic would have failed. After convergence the network returned to a fully healthy state with no residual fault.

Root cause: A coordinated FRR daemon restart across the entire topology caused a brief (~2 s) BGP convergence gap that interrupted cross-rack connectivity; the fabric self-recovered and requires no corrective action.

Corrective fix: No change needed. If FRR restarts recur unexpectedly, investigate why all daemons restarted at the same instant (supervisor crash, manual intervention, or orchestration event) and consider enabling BGP Graceful Restart to eliminate the forwarding blackout during future FRR restarts.
=== END ===